Roadmap: Landscaping Maintenance App
Phase 0 — Project Setup and “No Backtracking” Foundations

Goal: Create a skeleton where later work plugs in cleanly.

Session 0.1 — Repo + tooling scaffolding

Monorepo folders (backend, frontend)

Shared .env.example files

Backend:

Express app skeleton

Central config loader + env validation

Central error handler + request logging

Frontend:

Vite + React

Routing + layout shell

Mobile-first base components

Why this prevents later edits: all future code lands in well-defined module folders.

Session 0.2 — Database + migrations framework

Postgres connection layer

Migration tool (e.g., knex or drizzle migrations)

Seed: only for dev

Health endpoint checks DB connectivity

Lock-in decisions here: DB conventions, UUIDs, timestamps (UTC), soft-delete patterns.

Phase 1 — Canonical Data Model + Permissions (Build Once)

Goal: Define the schema and access control so we never redesign later.

Session 1.1 — Migrations for all core tables (complete schema up-front)

Create migrations for:

users (manager/admin/crew_lead)

workers

crews

crew_memberships (effective dating)

crew_lead_assignments

sites (includes crew-visible access notes)

site_contacts

schedule_assignments

visit_reports

report_photos (metadata only)

email_drafts

schedule_access_pin (single global PIN, hashed)

audit_log (optional but smart)

Why this prevents backtracking: we include the full end-state schema now, even if we don’t expose all screens yet.

Session 1.2 — Auth + RBAC + route guards

Login/logout/me

Password hashing

Role middleware (requireRole, requireCrewLead, etc.)

“Public schedule token” middleware for PIN access:

POST /api/pin/verify returns short-lived JWT

Policy layer:

Crew public token can only hit public schedule endpoints

Crew leads can only report against assignments for crews they lead

No-backtracking design: implement permission checks as reusable policy functions now.

Phase 2 — Scheduling API (No UI Yet)

Goal: Make schedule data solid before drag/drop UI.

Session 2.1 — Schedule read endpoints (public + crew lead + manager)

GET /api/public/schedule (PIN token)

GET /api/lead/schedule

GET /api/admin/schedule

Includes:

address + crew_visible_access_notes

crew roster for that day (resolved from memberships)

assignment ordering

Session 2.2 — Schedule write endpoints (manager/admin)

create assignment

edit assignment

delete assignment (soft delete recommended)

reorder within day

move between day/crew

No-backtracking trick: define a single “ScheduleService” that both API and later UI depend on.

Phase 3 — Public Schedule UI + PIN Gate (Crew Usability First)

Goal: Ship something crews can use early, with minimal risk.

Session 3.1 — PIN gate + token storage

PIN entry screen

Store scheduleView JWT securely (httpOnly cookie if possible; otherwise in memory + refresh)

Lock screen timeout / re-enter PIN after X hours

Session 3.2 — Public Schedule viewer (mobile-first)

Today + Week toggle

Crew selector (if needed)

Assignment cards show:

site/job label

address

access notes (gate/parking)

crew list

time window

manager public note

No backtracking: component architecture built for reuse by crew lead schedule later.

Phase 4 — Crew Lead Reporting + Timer (Core Value)

Goal: Enable reports + photos + man-hours, tied to schedule assignments.

Session 4.1 — Crew lead login UI + schedule view reuse

Crew lead login screen

Same schedule UI as crew, but cards have “Open job”

Session 4.2 — Timer endpoints + reliable state

Backend:

POST /lead/assignments/:id/timer/start

POST /lead/assignments/:id/timer/stop
Rules:

Only one active timer per crew lead (optional)

Prevent double-start

Store start/end events

Frontend:

Start/Stop button

Offline-safe queue (optional in MVP; add in Phase 9 if needed)

Session 4.3 — Report submission + man-hours calculation

Backend:

Create report for assignment

Store:

work_performed, WIP, issues

started_at, ended_at, duration

worker_count_at_visit (snapshotted)

total_man_minutes (snapshotted)
Logic:

worker_count = crew roster count for that assignment date (membership resolved)

man_minutes = duration_minutes * worker_count

Frontend:

Report form

Submit report

Basic validation + autosave draft locally (optional)

No backtracking: store snapshot fields so later membership changes don’t alter historical man-hours.

Phase 5 — Photo Upload + 14-Day Auto Deletion

Goal: Photos work reliably and don’t bloat your system.

Session 5.1 — Object storage integration (S3-compatible)

Backend:

presigned upload endpoint

confirm endpoint writes metadata

store expires_at = uploaded_at + 14 days

Infra:

Bucket lifecycle rule deletes after 14 days

Frontend:

multi-photo upload

progress indicator

retry on failure

No backtracking: photos are abstracted behind StorageService so changing providers later doesn’t touch business logic.

Phase 6 — Manager Console MVP (Manage Without Chaos)

Goal: Give you control surfaces that everything else depends on.

Session 6.1 — Manager dashboard (read-only first)

Today’s schedule

Reports submitted today

Missing reports list (assignments where requires_report = true and no report by end of day)

Session 6.2 — Crews + Workers management UI

CRUD workers

CRUD crews

assign workers to crews (with effective dates)

assign crew leads to crews

Session 6.3 — Sites master list UI (manager/admin only)

CRUD sites

edit crew-visible access notes

edit manager-private notes

CRUD site contacts (admin/manager only)

contract fields (admin/manager only)

No backtracking: master list is separate from what crews see; crews only see “assignment view model”.

Phase 7 — Drag-and-Drop Schedule Builder (Manager Only)

Goal: The flagship scheduling tool, built on already-solid APIs.

Session 7.1 — Weekly grid UI

Rows = crews

Columns = days

Cards = assignments

Session 7.2 — Drag/drop reorder + move

Reorder within crew/day

Drag to other day

Drag to other crew

Optimistic UI updates + rollback on failure

Session 7.3 — Assignment editor drawer

time window

public note

private note

requires_report toggle

job label override

No backtracking: we already built schedule write endpoints in Phase 2, so UI is “just a client.”

Phase 8 — AI Email Drafting + Manager Approval + Sending

Goal: Professional client comms with zero automatic sending.

Session 8.1 — Template system (editable by manager)

Backend:

email_templates table (or a simple “settings” table)

Default template: subject + body instructions + tone

Manager can edit templates from UI

Session 8.2 — Generate AI draft from report

Backend:

/admin/reports/:id/email-draft/generate

Strict prompt: rewrite only, no invention

Includes placeholders for photos list

Creates email_drafts record

Session 8.3 — Manager edit + approve/send UI

Draft editor (subject + body)

Shows report content and photos side-by-side

Manager can edit the underlying report text (per your requirement) before generating/sending

Send email (provider integration)

Log sent status + recipients

No backtracking: AI behavior controlled via templates/settings, not hard-coded prompts.

Phase 9 — Hardening for Field Reality (Offline, Reliability, Audit)

Goal: Make it survive real crews and bad signal.

Session 9.1 — PWA enhancements

Add to home screen

Offline cache for schedule

Background sync for report drafts / queued uploads (where feasible)

Session 9.2 — Audit log + change history

Log schedule edits, report edits, email sends, PIN rotations

Manager can see “who changed what”

Session 9.3 — Operational safeguards

Rate limits (PIN attempts, login)

Image upload limits and compression

Backups strategy for DB

Phase 10 — Reporting, Search, and Ops Intelligence

Goal: Turn data into management power.

Man-hours per site/week/month

Time on site trends

Report completeness

Export CSV/PDF

Search reports by site/date/crew/keyword

Build Order Summary (minimal backtracking)

Full schema + permissions (done once)

Schedule APIs (done once)

Crew schedule UI + PIN

Crew lead reporting + timer + man-hours snapshot

Photos + lifecycle deletion

Manager CRUD (sites/crews/workers)

Drag/drop schedule builder

AI drafts + manager approval + email send

Hardening (offline, audit, rate limits)

Analytics/reporting

Key “Anti-Backtracking” Design Choices

Full end-state DB schema early (even if UI comes later)

Central policy layer for permissions

Services: ScheduleService, StorageService, EmailService, AiDraftService

Snapshot worker_count_at_visit and total_man_minutes in reports (so historical numbers don’t change)

AI prompts stored in DB templates/settings (so you change behavior without code edits)

Schedule is the only entry point for reports (no site browsing
